WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.150 --> 00:00:01.230
- Welcome back, y'all.

00:00:01.230 --> 00:00:03.450
We are here with the cast of
the Super Mario Brothers Movie

00:00:03.450 --> 00:00:05.511
which is in theaters everywhere in April.

00:00:05.511 --> 00:00:06.663
We got Chris Pratt,

00:00:06.663 --> 00:00:07.642
Charlie Day,

00:00:07.642 --> 00:00:08.571
Keegan-Michael Key,

00:00:08.571 --> 00:00:09.404
and Jack Black.

00:00:09.404 --> 00:00:10.914
(audience cheers and applauds)

00:00:10.914 --> 00:00:13.680
So, you've all been here
before except for Bowser.

00:00:13.680 --> 00:00:15.346
Except for Jack.
- Oh my God. It's my firsties.

00:00:15.346 --> 00:00:16.260
- It's your first.
- Jack's first time.

00:00:16.260 --> 00:00:17.100
- But we've partied before.

00:00:17.100 --> 00:00:18.348
We did a charity thing together and-

00:00:18.348 --> 00:00:20.333
- And the first time,
you don't even remember.

00:00:20.333 --> 00:00:23.820
I sat next to you at the...

00:00:23.820 --> 00:00:24.870
I think I was still on Idol.

00:00:24.870 --> 00:00:26.087
- Oh yeah.

00:00:26.087 --> 00:00:28.260
- But it was whenever Michael
Jackson accepted the award

00:00:28.260 --> 00:00:29.093
that wasn't an award.

00:00:29.093 --> 00:00:29.926
- Oh yeah!

00:00:29.926 --> 00:00:30.759
- And it was like, "Happy Birthday"

00:00:30.759 --> 00:00:31.952
and he said, "Thank you
for being the Artist

00:00:31.952 --> 00:00:32.785
of the Millennium."

00:00:32.785 --> 00:00:34.203
And everyone was like, "Hmm?"

00:00:34.203 --> 00:00:36.420
- It was a crazy moment that we shared.

00:00:36.420 --> 00:00:37.573
- [Kelly] That was at
that one too, anyway.

00:00:37.573 --> 00:00:38.406
- I remember that.

00:00:38.406 --> 00:00:40.470
- [Kelly] I remember sitting
next to you 'cause I love you.

00:00:40.470 --> 00:00:41.640
- Aww. Feeling's mutch.

00:00:41.640 --> 00:00:42.750
- But anyway, yeah, I just-

00:00:42.750 --> 00:00:44.220
- Hey, you got pipes for days.

00:00:44.220 --> 00:00:45.930
We should do a duet one of these days.

00:00:45.930 --> 00:00:47.322
- I want to!

00:00:47.322 --> 00:00:49.318
(audience cheers and applauds)

00:00:49.318 --> 00:00:52.830
You should've been my partner
on the Kellyoke today.

00:00:52.830 --> 00:00:54.142
You would've killed it. It was like-

00:00:54.142 --> 00:00:54.975
- [Jack] Let's do this.

00:00:54.975 --> 00:00:55.808
- I shredded.

00:00:55.808 --> 00:00:57.210
So, basically 'cause my voice is shot

00:00:57.210 --> 00:00:58.944
but I actually love that
y'all are all musical.

00:00:58.944 --> 00:01:01.260
Everyone sitting here is musical.

00:01:01.260 --> 00:01:03.720
I mean, Tenacious D, obviously.

00:01:03.720 --> 00:01:04.720
- Little rock opera.

00:01:05.832 --> 00:01:06.960
(audience applauds)

00:01:06.960 --> 00:01:09.000
- Was that your first
love when you were a kid?

00:01:09.000 --> 00:01:10.590
Did you think you would...

00:01:10.590 --> 00:01:12.000
- No, I was a real ham

00:01:12.000 --> 00:01:14.670
and it didn't matter if it was
singing or acting, whatever.

00:01:14.670 --> 00:01:16.290
I just liked to get up in front of a crowd

00:01:16.290 --> 00:01:18.480
and I was doing both from an early age.

00:01:18.480 --> 00:01:20.048
I was singing and acting.

00:01:20.048 --> 00:01:21.560
Yeah. Putting on a show.

00:01:21.560 --> 00:01:23.070
- [Kelly] I love that.
- Didn't really matter.

00:01:23.070 --> 00:01:24.214
Old school showman.

00:01:24.214 --> 00:01:25.260
(Kelly laughs)

00:01:25.260 --> 00:01:26.430
- [Kelly] Well, Keegan, you're the same.

00:01:26.430 --> 00:01:29.250
You do musical theater,
you do all the things.

00:01:29.250 --> 00:01:31.440
Was your first love
music or was it acting?

00:01:31.440 --> 00:01:34.740
- No, the first thing I wanted
to be was a veterinarian.

00:01:34.740 --> 00:01:35.573
- [Kelly] Are you serious?

00:01:35.573 --> 00:01:37.260
- Yes. I love animals so much.

00:01:37.260 --> 00:01:39.688
I used to sit with my dad
and watch Jacques Cousteau's

00:01:39.688 --> 00:01:42.870
or Marlin Perkin's Wild Kingdom.

00:01:42.870 --> 00:01:46.680
Anybody who's my age and
knows what I'm talking about.

00:01:46.680 --> 00:01:48.690
But, at any rate, I wanted
to be a veterinarian

00:01:48.690 --> 00:01:50.910
but then I got into theater
as I got into high school

00:01:50.910 --> 00:01:52.487
and then, like most people in high school,

00:01:52.487 --> 00:01:54.960
a lot of people start in musical theater.

00:01:54.960 --> 00:01:56.626
- Yeah.
- [Key] So I did that,

00:01:56.626 --> 00:01:57.750
and then just recently in my career,

00:01:57.750 --> 00:01:59.903
that's the thing I've been kind of doing.

00:01:59.903 --> 00:02:00.850
I think I'm-
- [Kelly] You're great at it.

00:02:00.850 --> 00:02:01.830
- I think I've sung in the last four

00:02:01.830 --> 00:02:03.270
of the last six things I've worked on.

00:02:03.270 --> 00:02:04.410
- I know, that's what I'm saying.

00:02:04.410 --> 00:02:05.243
You're incredible.

00:02:05.243 --> 00:02:06.720
I always wonder what was the first love?

00:02:06.720 --> 00:02:08.130
I love asking people.

00:02:08.130 --> 00:02:11.190
You play seven instruments, I feel like.

00:02:11.190 --> 00:02:12.480
You play a lot of instruments.

00:02:12.480 --> 00:02:13.426
- Yeah.

00:02:13.426 --> 00:02:15.821
When I was in eighth
grade or seventh grade,

00:02:15.821 --> 00:02:19.080
I was in school band
and I played trombone.

00:02:19.080 --> 00:02:21.990
And I had a sweet sweatshirt
that would say your name

00:02:21.990 --> 00:02:22.823
and your instrument.

00:02:22.823 --> 00:02:25.357
So, I had this sweatshirt
that said "Charlie Trombone."

00:02:25.357 --> 00:02:26.190
- "Charlie Trombone."

00:02:26.190 --> 00:02:29.147
- Which became my nickname
which was cool with everyone

00:02:29.147 --> 00:02:30.765
except girls.

00:02:30.765 --> 00:02:33.600
(everyone laughs)

00:02:33.600 --> 00:02:35.266
And so I gave up the trombone.

00:02:35.266 --> 00:02:37.290
(everyone laughs)

00:02:37.290 --> 00:02:38.123
That's how I-

00:02:38.123 --> 00:02:39.030
- Women killed the dream.

00:02:39.030 --> 00:02:39.937
- Yeah, you know.

00:02:39.937 --> 00:02:42.450
- You're going in my phone
as "Charlie Trombone."

00:02:42.450 --> 00:02:43.283
- [Charlie] Okay. That's cool.

00:02:43.283 --> 00:02:44.327
That's good by me.

00:02:44.327 --> 00:02:45.900
- [Jack] That's pretty awesome.

00:02:45.900 --> 00:02:48.240
- Time to hit "edit"
in our contacts, yeah.

00:02:48.240 --> 00:02:49.890
- [Kelly] I know you play guitar.

00:02:49.890 --> 00:02:50.723
- [Chris] I do. Yeah.

00:02:50.723 --> 00:02:51.558
- You played in Parks and Rec.

00:02:51.558 --> 00:02:53.790
Was music a first love of yours?

00:02:53.790 --> 00:02:55.309
- Well, I always really loved music.

00:02:55.309 --> 00:02:58.484
I had a friend growing up,
Josh, who played guitar

00:02:58.484 --> 00:03:02.340
and when I decided to
go to community college

00:03:02.340 --> 00:03:04.230
after high school I was
looking through the classes

00:03:04.230 --> 00:03:06.545
and there was theater
and there was guitar.

00:03:06.545 --> 00:03:08.670
And so I was like, "I'm
gonna try those two out,"

00:03:08.670 --> 00:03:10.777
and I made it one semester in college.

00:03:10.777 --> 00:03:15.774
And by the third or fourth
day of playing the guitar,

00:03:15.774 --> 00:03:17.767
I learned like three
chords and I was like,

00:03:17.767 --> 00:03:19.218
"I'm done with school."

00:03:19.218 --> 00:03:21.222
(Kelly laughs)

00:03:21.222 --> 00:03:22.055
"I'm gonna go-"

00:03:22.055 --> 00:03:22.888
- You're my daughter.

00:03:22.888 --> 00:03:24.079
- [Chris] And sit and play music

00:03:24.079 --> 00:03:27.180
in the quad at the school
with my guitar case open

00:03:27.180 --> 00:03:28.829
and get money like they do.

00:03:28.829 --> 00:03:30.165
(Key laughs)
And I started making money.

00:03:30.165 --> 00:03:32.723
And I basically dropped out
of school and just played

00:03:32.723 --> 00:03:33.770
guitar.
- To busk. Okay.

00:03:33.770 --> 00:03:35.100
- To busk for a little bit.

00:03:35.100 --> 00:03:36.648
- I didn't know that.

00:03:36.648 --> 00:03:38.400
That's a good one.

00:03:38.400 --> 00:03:39.930
- This is my daughter.

00:03:39.930 --> 00:03:41.370
I'm like, "Oh, do you wanna take lessons?"

00:03:41.370 --> 00:03:43.036
'Cause she'll start playing.
She goes, "I already know how."

00:03:43.036 --> 00:03:45.114
And I'm like, "Okay, no you don't."

00:03:45.114 --> 00:03:46.620
(everyone laughs)

00:03:46.620 --> 00:03:48.450
I'm not the participation ribbon mom.

00:03:48.450 --> 00:03:50.250
I'm like, "No, you don't."

00:03:50.250 --> 00:03:51.840
I'm like, you gotta really spell it out.

00:03:51.840 --> 00:03:53.580
They're very confident
though at a young age

00:03:53.580 --> 00:03:55.530
and just think, yeah, you're
not playing Beethoven.

00:03:55.530 --> 00:03:56.837
It was like (imitates
pressing piano keys).

00:03:56.837 --> 00:03:57.966
(audience cheers and applauds)

00:03:57.966 --> 00:03:59.850
We're back with Chris Pratt, Charlie Day,

00:03:59.850 --> 00:04:01.530
Keegan-Michael Key, and Jack Black.

00:04:01.530 --> 00:04:02.730
Y'all give it up.

00:04:02.730 --> 00:04:03.563
(audience applauds)

00:04:03.563 --> 00:04:05.490
Together they star the
Super Mario Brothers Movie

00:04:05.490 --> 00:04:07.263
in theaters everywhere in April.

00:04:07.263 --> 00:04:09.780
All right, so, Keegan, you
started out doing improv,

00:04:09.780 --> 00:04:12.810
so what's a memory that will
unfortunately stay with you?

00:04:12.810 --> 00:04:14.460
- Well, one thing I
think that's interesting,

00:04:14.460 --> 00:04:16.050
especially the rest of these guys

00:04:16.050 --> 00:04:17.370
who've improvised, you know?

00:04:17.370 --> 00:04:20.400
Improvising with other people
is one of the great joys

00:04:20.400 --> 00:04:21.233
of my life.

00:04:21.233 --> 00:04:22.066
- The energy of it.

00:04:22.066 --> 00:04:23.550
- [Key] The energy of it,
and the give and take of it,

00:04:23.550 --> 00:04:24.903
and what we call "Yes, and-ing."

00:04:24.903 --> 00:04:27.330
The hardest part is that
you can't always control

00:04:27.330 --> 00:04:28.515
what the audience is gonna say.

00:04:28.515 --> 00:04:30.690
So sometimes you'll ask for a suggestion

00:04:30.690 --> 00:04:33.150
and there's one thing that
no matter what you do,

00:04:33.150 --> 00:04:35.317
if you ask for a
profession, you could say,

00:04:35.317 --> 00:04:38.587
"So, Ladies and gentlemen,
just gimme a date in history."

00:04:38.587 --> 00:04:39.727
"1776!"

00:04:39.727 --> 00:04:40.770
"Okay, great, that's wonderful.

00:04:40.770 --> 00:04:42.847
Can I have a suggestion of an occupation?"

00:04:42.847 --> 00:04:44.484
"Proctologist!"

00:04:44.484 --> 00:04:45.337
- Every time.

00:04:45.337 --> 00:04:48.690
- "Great. What if we try
a different occupation?"

00:04:48.690 --> 00:04:50.223
Every time you ask it'll just be like,

00:04:50.223 --> 00:04:51.607
"Can I get a profession?"

00:04:51.607 --> 00:04:53.122
"Proctologist!"

00:04:53.122 --> 00:04:55.800
Every audience, no matter where you are.

00:04:55.800 --> 00:04:56.880
First suggestion.

00:04:56.880 --> 00:04:57.713
Proctologist.

00:04:57.713 --> 00:04:58.650
- [Kelly] Says a lot about this audience.

00:04:58.650 --> 00:05:00.577
- Says a lot about our
society, doesn't it?

00:05:00.577 --> 00:05:02.107
"Okay, how about not proctologist."

00:05:02.107 --> 00:05:03.900
"Gynecologist!" "Okay. All right.'

00:05:03.900 --> 00:05:04.788
- Okay, cool.

00:05:04.788 --> 00:05:05.943
Switching it up.

00:05:07.410 --> 00:05:08.843
- They're into science which is cool.

00:05:10.053 --> 00:05:10.886
[Key Laughs]
- They're the anatomy.

00:05:10.886 --> 00:05:12.097
- Well, 'cause everyone's thinking,

00:05:12.097 --> 00:05:13.890
"What's the funniest thing I can say?"

00:05:13.890 --> 00:05:16.008
and of course it goes
straight to the butt.

00:05:16.008 --> 00:05:18.593
(Kelly laughs)
- [Key] Because poop is funny.

00:05:18.593 --> 00:05:19.800
Poop is funny.

00:05:19.800 --> 00:05:21.208
- [Kelly] Oh my God. Says my six year old.

00:05:21.208 --> 00:05:23.400
So, what about you, Chris?

00:05:23.400 --> 00:05:25.646
Any early onstage memories
that you'd rather forget?

00:05:25.646 --> 00:05:30.646
- Oh man, I remember doing
standup comedy in Seattle

00:05:30.840 --> 00:05:32.070
at this little place called Giggles.

00:05:32.070 --> 00:05:34.040
It was like an open mic kind of thing.

00:05:34.040 --> 00:05:35.490
And I was like the...

00:05:35.490 --> 00:05:37.830
I was funny in high school
people thought I was funny

00:05:37.830 --> 00:05:40.530
and I used to make people
laugh but getting up on a stage

00:05:40.530 --> 00:05:44.100
and doing it's a totally
different scenario.

00:05:44.100 --> 00:05:45.372
- [Kelly] Like a planned hilarious moment.

00:05:45.372 --> 00:05:46.590
- [Chris] Yeah, because you can be funny

00:05:46.590 --> 00:05:48.570
around your friends because
you catch 'em off guard.

00:05:48.570 --> 00:05:50.700
You're like, "I wasn't expecting you

00:05:50.700 --> 00:05:52.170
to say a joke just now."

00:05:52.170 --> 00:05:53.003
- Right, right, right.

00:05:53.003 --> 00:05:55.770
- But taking away the surprise
and being like, "Say a joke."

00:05:55.770 --> 00:05:56.783
- Right, right right.

00:05:57.816 --> 00:05:58.680
- You're like, "I don't know how."

00:05:58.680 --> 00:06:01.950
So I had a whole list of various jokes

00:06:01.950 --> 00:06:04.410
that I thought I was gonna
go through and then my guitar

00:06:04.410 --> 00:06:05.986
and I was gonna do a
whole bit and I was okay.

00:06:05.986 --> 00:06:07.710
But then I made the mistake

00:06:07.710 --> 00:06:09.300
of leaving my guitar case up there

00:06:09.300 --> 00:06:12.720
with my list of jokes and
the next comic came up

00:06:12.720 --> 00:06:13.985
and took my list out and just-

00:06:13.985 --> 00:06:15.000
- Ripped.

00:06:15.000 --> 00:06:16.705
- Destroyed me.

00:06:16.705 --> 00:06:17.559
- [Key] Are you kidding me?
- Just made so much fun of me

00:06:17.559 --> 00:06:18.800
and my list of jokes.

00:06:18.800 --> 00:06:20.873
- Oh no, Chris.

00:06:20.873 --> 00:06:21.706
- [Kelly] I mean, kind-

00:06:21.706 --> 00:06:22.539
- Where are you now?

00:06:22.539 --> 00:06:23.965
Where are you now?

00:06:23.965 --> 00:06:26.114
(everyone laughs)

00:06:26.114 --> 00:06:27.462
- Still at Giggles.

00:06:27.462 --> 00:06:28.380
- Yeah, I think he's still at Giggles.

00:06:28.380 --> 00:06:30.168
- Carrot Top, you son of a!

00:06:30.168 --> 00:06:31.843
(everyone laughs)

00:06:31.843 --> 00:06:33.923
- It was Jerry Seinfeld.

00:06:33.923 --> 00:06:38.432
- Looking back though
it's kinda funny. (laughs)

00:06:38.432 --> 00:06:40.261
- You gotta take some lumps,
I'll tell you. You gotta take-

00:06:40.261 --> 00:06:41.970
- That's just material for days.

00:06:41.970 --> 00:06:43.435
Like, "Well, you just handed it to me."

00:06:43.435 --> 00:06:44.908
(everyone laughs)

00:06:44.908 --> 00:06:46.530
- Yeah, and it wasn't...

00:06:46.530 --> 00:06:48.510
There was a reason he went to the list.

00:06:48.510 --> 00:06:49.440
He wasn't being mean.

00:06:49.440 --> 00:06:50.483
My stuff was so bad that
he had to make fun of it.

00:06:50.483 --> 00:06:53.580
- It is interesting
though when people think

00:06:53.580 --> 00:06:56.050
that you're funny but it
doesn't translate always.

00:06:56.050 --> 00:06:58.680
Or you think your friend is very funny

00:06:58.680 --> 00:06:59.677
and they do something and you're like,

00:06:59.677 --> 00:07:00.810
"Oh, why didn't that translate?"

00:07:00.810 --> 00:07:02.400
That is a hard thing to do.

00:07:02.400 --> 00:07:03.450
- Yeah.
- [Kelly] I think, yeah.

00:07:03.450 --> 00:07:05.771
Jack, I heard that your first improv game,

00:07:05.771 --> 00:07:09.510
I hope this is true,
was at a Passover Seder.

00:07:09.510 --> 00:07:10.343
Is that true?
- Yup.

00:07:10.343 --> 00:07:13.590
- [Kelly] Okay, so just
how does that happen?

00:07:13.590 --> 00:07:16.192
Like, what kind of games
are played at that?

00:07:16.192 --> 00:07:18.390
- It was an amazing...

00:07:18.390 --> 00:07:20.070
I remember we had the Passover Seder

00:07:20.070 --> 00:07:23.214
at a friend's house and
she was an amazing lady.

00:07:23.214 --> 00:07:25.680
She was a Holocaust survivor, actually.

00:07:25.680 --> 00:07:28.350
And she had this incredible spirit

00:07:28.350 --> 00:07:33.350
and energy of creativity
and joy and she said,

00:07:33.397 --> 00:07:35.010
"Now we're done with the Seder.

00:07:35.010 --> 00:07:37.080
We're gonna play a freeze game.

00:07:37.080 --> 00:07:39.390
Come on, everyone come
to the living room."

00:07:39.390 --> 00:07:42.030
And we played these Viola Spolin games.

00:07:42.030 --> 00:07:46.080
They're improv games and I was so into it.

00:07:46.080 --> 00:07:48.930
I just wanted to be up
onstage the whole time

00:07:48.930 --> 00:07:51.270
in this little living
room and getting laughs

00:07:51.270 --> 00:07:53.160
and that's where I first caught the bug.

00:07:53.160 --> 00:07:53.993
Yeah.

00:07:53.993 --> 00:07:55.065
- [Kelly] That energy is awesome.

00:07:55.065 --> 00:07:57.480
I don't know it in the comedic way,

00:07:57.480 --> 00:07:59.250
but just playing on stage, musically,

00:07:59.250 --> 00:08:01.710
you have nights when
people are really into it

00:08:01.710 --> 00:08:03.067
and you're feeding off of
it and then you're like,

00:08:03.067 --> 00:08:05.670
"Wow, they must have thought
somebody else was playing

00:08:05.670 --> 00:08:06.510
like Pink."

00:08:06.510 --> 00:08:08.347
And you have nights where it's just like,

00:08:08.347 --> 00:08:09.635
"Oh, it's not going like I thought."

00:08:09.635 --> 00:08:12.570
And you feed off of that
energy, I feel like.

00:08:12.570 --> 00:08:14.580
- Yeah, you know it when you try it,

00:08:14.580 --> 00:08:16.710
if you feel that electricity.

00:08:16.710 --> 00:08:19.680
You can feel it in a
lot of different games.

00:08:19.680 --> 00:08:22.170
What's the one where you you
try to guess what the person?

00:08:22.170 --> 00:08:23.439
The movie?

00:08:23.439 --> 00:08:24.272
- Charades.

00:08:24.272 --> 00:08:25.105
- Charades.

00:08:25.105 --> 00:08:27.690
You know, any kind of game that
that requires you to get up

00:08:27.690 --> 00:08:29.103
and put on a little show.

00:08:30.240 --> 00:08:31.803
You can feel that.

00:08:32.970 --> 00:08:35.460
I could play charades for hours
and hours when I was a kid

00:08:35.460 --> 00:08:37.350
because it was so fun
to put on a little show.

00:08:37.350 --> 00:08:39.683
- And, because you couldn't
think of the word "charades,"

00:08:39.683 --> 00:08:42.075
the game would probably go really long.

00:08:42.075 --> 00:08:43.837
(everyone laughs)

00:08:43.837 --> 00:08:45.508
- [Kelly] Took so long.

00:08:45.508 --> 00:08:47.412
(Key stammers)
- [Chris] Game! Game!

00:08:47.412 --> 00:08:49.055
- [Kelly] It's like...
- [Charlie] Game!

00:08:49.055 --> 00:08:50.041
Two words!

00:08:50.041 --> 00:08:52.050
(Key stammers)
Two syllables.

00:08:52.050 --> 00:08:53.640
- You sit.

00:08:53.640 --> 00:08:55.080
Can't talk.

00:08:55.080 --> 00:08:56.650
- It's like grunting. (laughs)

00:08:56.650 --> 00:08:57.870
- Okay, a chair.

00:08:57.870 --> 00:08:59.120
What's the next syllable?

00:09:00.835 --> 00:09:01.858
(Key imitates drinking sound)

00:09:01.858 --> 00:09:02.853
Oh yeah!

00:09:04.416 --> 00:09:05.249
(Kelly laughs)

00:09:05.249 --> 00:09:07.230
- It's make fun of Jack day.

00:09:07.230 --> 00:09:09.900
Everything I do, you guys
are trying to sabotage me.

00:09:09.900 --> 00:09:11.577
- Jack, we're not. Jack.

00:09:11.577 --> 00:09:13.560
- They left you hanging with the costume.

00:09:13.560 --> 00:09:14.820
- All I'm doing is trying to think

00:09:14.820 --> 00:09:16.629
of how I'm gonna get
you guys back someday.

00:09:16.629 --> 00:09:18.990
(everyone laughs)

00:09:18.990 --> 00:09:20.430
- [Kelly] Who's really winning though?

00:09:20.430 --> 00:09:21.450
I think you're really winning.

00:09:21.450 --> 00:09:22.350
- He is winning.

00:09:22.350 --> 00:09:23.490
- Charlie, I have to ask,

00:09:23.490 --> 00:09:25.350
what was your first time
in front of an audience?

00:09:25.350 --> 00:09:26.477
- Oh man, yeah.

00:09:26.477 --> 00:09:29.160
Four years old and I got to-

00:09:29.160 --> 00:09:29.993
- [Kelly] Wow, that's young.

00:09:29.993 --> 00:09:31.830
- To walk out on stage of a school play

00:09:31.830 --> 00:09:34.020
holding the sign that said "Intermission."

00:09:34.020 --> 00:09:36.853
(everyone laughs)

00:09:38.100 --> 00:09:41.010
- And I thought to
myself, "I'm gonna crush.

00:09:41.010 --> 00:09:42.060
I'm gonna walk out there.

00:09:42.060 --> 00:09:43.650
I'm gonna hold this thing upside down."

00:09:43.650 --> 00:09:44.940
But I didn't tell anybody.

00:09:44.940 --> 00:09:46.170
I walk out, hold it upside down

00:09:46.170 --> 00:09:48.914
and get the big huge "aw,
isn't that cute" laugh.

00:09:48.914 --> 00:09:52.720
And I thought, "I got what it takes, man."

00:09:52.720 --> 00:09:54.029
- [Chris] That's the move.`

00:09:54.029 --> 00:09:55.410
- I can do this. I can do this.

00:09:55.410 --> 00:09:56.370
- They're not gonna remember the play

00:09:56.370 --> 00:09:58.110
but they remember the kid
with the intermission sign.

00:09:58.110 --> 00:09:59.520
- It was my best performance.

00:09:59.520 --> 00:10:00.693
It's downhill since then.

00:10:00.693 --> 00:10:01.838
(Kelly laughs)

